Date: Tue, 20 Apr 93 10:19:02 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Singularity Studies

I originally said:

     > Your comment contradicts the definition of 'progress' I made.
> I explicitly said "I assume that 'progress' is measured by the
    > growth of human population". 
 
Evan Replies:

You did not define "progress".  To say that it is measured somehow in some
way is not to define it.  Many things can be measured in this way; please
clarify what you mean by "progress".  When I think of progress, I mean
such things as standard of living, e.g. educational opportunities, enough
food, health care if needed, etc.

I respond again:

Obviously I was not clear enough the last time.  It is a common
means of discussion to define a new term.  In this case I defined
a term ['progress'] which is not the same as [progress].  To
distinguish between the two I put single quotation marks around
the word.  The unquoted word has the common dictionary definition
you are thinking of.  The word with quotation marks has the
restricted definition of only considering the growth in population
aspect.  Thus whatever technical or social inventions that allow
population growth to happen are what contribute to the restricted
measure I defined.

Since this form of discussion was confusing, let me try to restate
it in a simpler form:  I have a hypothesis that human inventiveness
has been constant over history.  Some inventions allow for greater
populations to exist.  I deduce that population should exhibit
an accelerating growth rate proportional to the population then
present.  The data I observe are this:

Year		Population	Growth Rate	Ratio
		(billions)	(%/year)	(Rate/Pop)
10,000BC	0.004		0.004		1.0
5,000 BC	0.005		0.03		6.0
4,000 BC	0.007		0.07		10.0
3,000 BC	0.014		0.065		4.6
2,000 BC	0.027		0.061		2.3
1,000 BC	0.050		0.14		2.8
500 BC		0.100		0.11		1.1
0 AD		0.170		0.02		0.12
500 AD		0.190		0.07		0.4
1000 AD		0.265		0.09		0.3
1500 AD		0.425		0.25		0.6
1600 AD		0.545		0.11		0.2
1700 AD		0.610		0.39		0.6
1800 AD		0.900		0.59		0.7
1900 AD		1.625		0.86		0.5
1950 AD		2.500		1.82		0.7
2000 AD		6.200		???

Since 0 AD the ratio has held within a fairly narrow band (0.12
to 0.7) while the population has increased 36-fold.  When we take
out the effects of the fall of the Roman and Chinese empires
around 200 AD, the Black Death after 1350, and the importation
of diseases when the New World began to be settled, the variation
falls into a much narrower range.  The large bulge centered
around 6-7,000 years ago is caused by the introduction of
agriculture.  At the peak of the ratio it only represents
5,000 people per year added to the entire earth population.
It could be expected that the first major invention in this
period would cause a step-function like increase in population.
Later inventions would overlap in time as they take time to
spread through the world (after all, it was only recently
that the last hunter-gatherers stopped doing that)  If
we estimate that the population will be growing by about
100 million per year in the year 2000, then the ratio will
be 0.26.  This is within the range of the last 2000 years.

Additional Note:

A few days ago someone asked me for references, and I was not
able to supply one at the time.  I can now give the following:

"Although the world was already inhabited by the end of the
Paleolithic, the total population numbers were still low (11),
perhaps between 1 and 10 million"

Cavalli-Sforza, et al., Science v.259 p. 640, 29 Jan 93.

(11) J.N. Biraben, "Population" v4 #1 (1980); F. A. Hassan,
"Demographic Archeology" (Academic Press, New York, 1981).


Evan Reese says:

I am not saying your hypothesis is equivalent to that, but if
it cannot account for the self-admitted fact that it no longer applies to
the United States, and if it cannot explain why high population growth
areas such as India --among other places--are such backwaters,I think it
needs reexamining.  Why did the Greeks or the
Chinese not continue on the technological track they had started out on? 
Why have so many societies throughout history gone for centuries without
appearing to have invented very much at all? Many other questions abound.
 It may be that they will be answered when you define what you mean by
progress; but by my above definition, whatever utility your hypothesismight
have had is--has been--swamped by our present and increasing ability to
process and communicate information. 

I respond:

>From 1950 to 2000 AD, the world as a whole will have a population
growth rate of about 1.8% per year, while the US will average about
1.2%.  From 1900 to 1950 AD the world averaged 0.9% while the US
averaged 1.4%.  The big change was that the US restricted immigration.

India and China are able to sustain high population growth because
agricultural and medical improvements flow from the more developed
parts of the world.  I don't claim my hypothesis can describe small-
scale features of population change, such as immigration policy,
the baby boom, or the killing fields of Cambodia.  The really big
invention was the domestication of animals and plants.  Since then,
the productivity of agricultural crops has increased, but by small
steps.  I would list a few other really big inventions: the
harnessing of wind and water power (for mills and for ships), and
the combination of blast furnaces for making iron in quantity,
and using that iron in steam engines.  I would rate computers
in this category of invention, and probably the printing press
with movable type.

I would claim that the big inventions that have a large impact
on human population are not that great in number, and hence it
is quite possible for a culture to go for hundreds of years with
no significant change.  If we list some inventions in a category,
we could look at how they are spaced across human history.  There
have been 1.2 trillion person-years of history since the Neolithic.

In information technology, I would list writing, the printing press
and the computer as the big inventions.  These occurred about
40, 550, and 950 billion person-years through history.

In power technology, we have animal power, water and wind power,
and steam power, and soon to be PV power (nuclear still uses steam
in the process), and these occurred at 20 billion, 450, 750, and
1100 billion person yeras.  To me these seem evenly spaced.  If you
have alternate lists of inventions, let me know how they come out.

