I'm not sure what you'd do with just the names of my sexual contacts. It
would have to be some kind of confirmable list. (What, you want video tape?
;-> ) And confirmable that I didn't leave anyone off of it, before that
list became a valuable indicator on my AIDS risk (which is, I assume what
we are talking about here).

What your actual point is, I'm not absolutely sure. But, I'll take a stab
at it anyway:

Paraphrasing your point:
There are some conditions (in insurance and PPLs) that may be statistically
correlated with some factors (race, gender, sexual-preference, etc.), and
that a business would be stupid not to pay attention to these factors.

Reiterating my point:
Whether or not the above correlation is true, I'll try like hell to avoid
paying for something that happened to (or was done by) someone similar to
me in some (statistically significant) way. I'll either switch or reduce my
coverage depending on my options. They lose some of my money either way. 

Under the current unfree market system, I would complain to my
congress-critter (on heavy stock paper to add weight to my opinion) and if
enough people complained along with them they would get regulated.

So what looks like good business sense, may not be so good. It just depends
on what we (the consumer) let them get away with.


But that's just part of your point, you ask how does a company cover it's
risk, and if it can't, should the government should bail him out.

I think there should be an unknown risk category at higher rate, and if you
can prove that you (personally, not statistically) are a lower risk *then*
you get discounts on your rate. This should cover the company's risk. (No
bailout necessary.)

