Date: Tue, 20 Apr 93 19:48:45 GMT
From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: TECH: Report - Entropic Cost of Computation

Derek Zahn, in his excellent summary of the fundamental limits of
computation, laments:

> The explanation of [the entropic cost of bit erasure] depends on
> uniting the information- theoretic notion of entropy and the
> thermodynamic notion of entropy.
> Despite what some would have you believe, unless something miraculous
> has happened since 1990, the validity of that link is far from clear
> (and I'd be happy to debate any contrary view).

This is my attempt to wield the two sides of entropy into a unified
whole.  This problem concerned me a few years ago.  This was my
approach:

Prove the 2nd Law for Shannon/information theoretic entropy.  Relate the
Shannon entropy to the thermodynamic definition and derive the usual
equations of state.  Rewrite the thermodynamic state equations in terms
of bits and get the limits on computation.

In more detail, but omitting much maths gore:

Start with the Shannon definition for the entropy, S, of a macrostate:

  S = -k sum p ln p 

where k is Boltzmann's constant and p the probability of a microstate
(a complete specification of a system).  The sum is over all microstates
compatible with the macrostate you're interested in.  This definition
of entropy is the only one for which I've seen a water tight (ie
mathematically iron-clad) derivation of the 2nd Law second law of
thermodynamics (which says that entropy of an isolated system always
increases). [1]

Once we're satisfied with the 2nd Law, for systems in equilibrium, we
can demand that entropy is maximised.  This occurs when the probability
of each microstate being realised is equal.  Quantum mechanics provides
an upper limit to the number of microstates available to any macrostate. 
Call this limit W.  Then we have:

    p = 1/W

so:

    S = -k sum p ln p
      = -k W * 1/W ln (1/W)   (since there are W microstate to sum
                              over, each of probability 1/W)
      = k ln W

hence: 

    S = k ln W   (Boltzmann-Planck equation).

Suppose we have a gas of N molecules in a cavity, volume V.  The
dimensions of the cavity define the energy levels available for each
molecule.  As an approximation suppose the molecules are non-interacting
(a perfect classical gas - so we'll ignore fermions vs bosons).  The
macrostate of the gas is specified by giving the number of molecules
(N1, N2, N3, ...) that exist in each energy level (E1, E2, E3...).  The
number of microstates, W, for each macrostate is given by how many ways
the set of (N1, N2, ...) identical particles can be selected from N
particles.  Combinatorial theory gives the answer:

    W(N1, N2, ..)  =  N! / (N1! * N2! * ...)

Maximising W will maximise the entropy and find the equilibrium state
for the gas.  Maximising W forces a relationship between the occupancy
counts of:

    Ni / Nj  = exp -(Ei - Ej)/B,   where B is an unknown constant

This becomes the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution if we identify the
unknown quantity B with kT, where T is the temperature of the gas. 
There is no arbitrariness in this identification, because we can regard
this as the *definition* of temperature.

The total energy, E, of the gas is

  E = sum Ni * Ei
       i
The crux comes when we differentiate E with respect to S holding V and
N constant.  We get

   dE/dS = T

Allowing the volume, V, and number of molecules, N, to vary we get:

   dE = TdS - PdV + udN,   P = pressure, u = chemical potential

which describes the change in energy of a gas under infinitesimal
changes in pressure, additions/subtractions and volume.  When I was at
college this was the most central equation for thermodynamics.  To prove
anything, in an exam, start from this.  The reason why this is such a
universally applicable equation is because we've made almost no physical
assumptions in its derivation.  In applying it to a physical situation
the problem is in identifying the appropriate physical quantities that
correspond to 'temperature', 'entropy', etc.  Once that is done the
equation is almost certain to be obeyed.  To calculate the cost of
erasure we shall relate the information to entropy.

Information, I, associated with an event of probability, p, is often
defined as:

   I = -log2(p),   (unexpected events convey a lot of information)
     = - ln(p) / ln2

and   S = k <I> ln2,  <I> = expected information = sum p * -ln(p)/ln2
so    dS = k ln2 d<I>

So (finally!):

   dE >= TdS = kT ln2 d<I>
or 
   dE >= kT * ln2 * number of bits erased
   (equality only for reversible operations)

where dI is the change in expected information.  In the case of a one
bit register the information change (loss) is one bit.  So minimum
energy required for erasure of a bit is kT * ln2.  This derivation is,
IMO, completely rigorous and insensitive to the details of the computer,
unlike many of the derivations in the literature.  Equally applicable
to Drexler's rod-logic machine phase state, Babbage's cogs and gears or
our own brains.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk
                                      AS member (21/3/93)

[1]  The MWI of QM, appendix I.2, p122-3 eds BS DeWitt & N Graham - out
     of print!
     The only physical assumption made is that the transition matrices
     in physics are doubly stochastic (which is implied by unitarity). 
     Something which (almost) every physicist has absolute faith in. 
     Roughly equivalent to physics being reversible on the microscale. 
     I've only ever seen this information theoretic proof of the 2nd Law
     in H.Everett's (him of many-worlds fame/infamy) doctoral thesis. 
     Which is strange, because his thesis had very little to do with
     entropy.  And the derivation of the 2nd Law he gave was derived
     from the mathematical properties of f(x) = xlnx.  If anybody knows
     where else this approach has appeared I'd be very grateful.

